// Title: Linux Covert SSH Tunnel via GSocket
// Sigma ID: a7d2e5c9-9b6f-4e18-a1c7-8f3d6b2e5a90
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Ivan Saakov
// Date: 2026-05-10 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: command-and-control, T1572
// Description: Detects execution of gsocket, gs-netcat, or associated binaries that provide SSH-like access
//   over the Global Socket Relay Network (GSRN) without requiring an exposed TCP port. GSSocket
//   authenticates using a shared secret and routes traffic through a relay, making it invisible to
//   network-level monitoring tools that look for direct inbound connections. Attackers use it to
//   maintain persistent backdoor access on hosts behind NAT or strict firewalls.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use of gsocket by authorized administrators for secure remote access
//   - Security team using gsocket in an approved red team exercise
// References:
//   - https://github.com/hackerschoice/gsocket
//   - https://www.hackerschoice.com/gsocket
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/e5bac28da3655c33390db7ef1503db8cd3babb5f/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_gsocket_tunnel.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/gsocket" or FolderPath endswith "/gs-netcat" or FolderPath endswith "/gs-sftp" or FolderPath endswith "/gs-mount"
